239... <h3>Testing the tester: how each fix changed what the evaluator catches</h3>
240... <div class="table-wrap"><table>
241...   <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Buggy caught</th><th>False alarms</th><th>Order bugs (reverse / sort-flip / tie-break)</th><th>Full run</th></tr></thead>
242...   <tbody>
243...     <tr><td>eval-proto, multiset only</td><td>10 / 11</td><td>0 / 8</td><td><span class="bad">0</span> / 4 / 2 defects</td><td>8.4 s</td></tr>
244...     <tr><td>+ order rule</td><td>11 / 11</td><td>0 / 8</td><td><span class="ok">172 / 134 / 19</span> defects</td><td>8.4 s</td></tr>
245...     <tr><td>+ type-safe compare, platform binary</td><td>11 / 11</td><td>0 / 8</td><td>172 / 134 / 19 defects</td><td><span class="ok">1.7 s</span></td></tr>
246...   </tbody>
247... </table></div>
248... 
249... <h2 id="lessons">What we learned</h2>
250... <ol>
251...   <li><b>The big wins are generic.</b> Filter first, build strings late, gather only the columns that reach <code>OUT</code>, delete dead code, use TOPK. These gave 4–8.5× on q0001, q0004 and q0007. The self-join idioms win most on large data (20–47×), and they are the riskiest.</li>
252...   <li><b>Correctness may use constraints, never stats.</b> <code>customers</code> has no key, so assuming <code>c_id</code> is unique is a real bug, caught by the q0004 baseline.</li>
253...   <li><b>NULL semantics are the main trap.</b> HJOIN drops NULL keys but GROUP keeps them, and NULLIFE turns <code>''</code> into NULL.</li>
254...   <li><b>An operation may only run on a subset of the rows it originally ran on.</b> Removing work can only remove errors, which is allowed.</li>
255...   <li><b>Weak data hides bugs.</b> The example data passed two broken q0007 variants, and generated data missed a q0004 bug until literals from the programs were planted in it.</li>
256...   <li><b>Multiset comparison is too lenient</b> when the program sorts, and an order rule fixes it. <b>The output JSON has no types</b>, so BOOL must never equal INT, and only text in minivm's own number format may be compared as a number.</li>
257...   <li><b>Test the tester.</b> Zero defects proves nothing until seeded bugs are caught and honest-but-different optimizers pass.</li>
258...   <li><b>Measure the harness too.</b> The platform binary is 4.6× faster than the wrapper, which leaves the 2-minute budget roomy.</li>
259... </ol>
